  "content": {
    "id": "p11-q15-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "15",
    "prompt": "The number $999$ is clearly composite, since $999=9\\times111$. More impressive is the fact that $999$ is composite when viewed as a number in any base exceeding $9$. Prove this fact.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "In base $b>9$, $(999)_b=9(b^2+b+1)$. Both factors exceed $1$, so the number is composite.",
      "worked": "Let the base be an integer $b>9$. By place value,\n$$\\begin{align*}(999)_b&=9b^2+9b+9\\\\(999)_b&=9(b^2+b+1).\\end{align*}$$\nBoth $9$ and $b^2+b+1$ are integers greater than $1$. Therefore, $(999)_b$ is composite in every base exceeding $9$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 44
  },
